WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.180
They're the most dangerous extreme weather
phenomena.

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:10.960
Heat waves basically affect our bodies and
the ecosystems around us, and they send us

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:13.169
to the hospital or they kill us.

00:00:13.169 --> 00:00:16.200
This is Europe's first Chief Heat Officer.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:22.060
Her task is to find ways to protect citizens
from the dangerous effects of extreme heat.

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:26.571
As the climate warms and the frequency of
heat waves dramatically increases, cities

00:00:26.571 --> 00:00:29.390
are appointing people like her to limit the
dangers.

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:34.789
We talked to three of them to find out how
we can best deal with this new normal.

00:00:34.789 --> 00:00:40.010
What's coming our way and what can we do to
protect ourselves?

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:46.960
I started thinking of what I have to do in
three pillars.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:50.199
The first pillar has to do with raising awareness.

00:00:50.199 --> 00:00:54.039
How do we communicate and get people to take
this seriously?

00:00:54.039 --> 00:00:58.800
Many of us aren't aware of just how dangerous
heat can be because heat is a silent killer

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:03.980
– we don't see the effects of extreme heat
as visibly as the physical destruction brought

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:07.240
by hurricanes, floods and other natural disasters.

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:12.720
But extreme heat now kills up to half a million
people worldwide each year - that's more than

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:17.130
all other natural disasters that aren't due
to the temperature.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:20.990
And the problem is getting worse, especially
in cities, which are heating up at twice the

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:25.170
global average rate because they trap heat
more than non-urban areas.

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:32.290
This is going to be one of the coolest summers
that we're going to have for the rest of our

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:33.290
lives.

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:36.670
And that's not something that we should take
lightly.

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:42.890
We really have to understand that cities are
really hotspots.They're not going to be able

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:45.540
to be viable in a few decades.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:51.900
By 2100, cities across the world could warm
as much as 4.4°C on average.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:56.550
So how do officials best sound the alarm and
make sure people understand the gravity of

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:58.980
the situation?

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:02.630
One of the things that was done for raising
awareness, which I think is really a game

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:09.920
changer, we have been categorizing heat waves
for the first time in Athens this year.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:14.569
It's a pilot project that was started in Seville,
Spain and Athens, Greece and will be adopted

00:02:14.569 --> 00:02:16.180
by other cities soon.

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:20.569
It categorizes heat waves based on their effect
on human health – much like earthquakes

00:02:20.569 --> 00:02:23.019
get categorized based on their intensity.

00:02:23.019 --> 00:02:26.480
The idea is to help people prepare and respond
better.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:27.599
And that's not all.

00:02:27.599 --> 00:02:30.209
Heat waves will also be named in the future.

00:02:30.209 --> 00:02:34.780
Just like Hurricane Katrina immediately evokes
these images in your head and makes it easier

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:39.840
to remember the disaster, so may Heat Wave
Zoe – the world’s first-ever heat event

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:49.290
to officially be named in Seville at the end
of July.

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:53.459
Once it's clear that a heat wave is especially
dangerous, information on how to stay safe

00:02:53.459 --> 00:02:55.939
can be spread to those who are most endangered.

00:02:55.939 --> 00:03:00.439
Messaging around heat waves is really critical
because there's a lot people can do with their

00:03:00.439 --> 00:03:03.260
own behavior to keep them safer.

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:08.200
The city of Athens even developed an extreme
heat app that's now also being used in Milan,

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:09.380
Paris and Rotterdam.

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:16.010
We have an application called Extrema Global
that shows you your personalized risk, depending

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:23.069
where you are in the city and your age and
your gender and whether you have preexisting

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:24.650
conditions.

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:28.680
And it also tells you on the map where to
go to take cover, where the cool spots are

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:31.920
around you.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:36.790
In a similar effort, Africa's first heat officer
is currently creating a heat map for the city

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:38.400
of Freetown.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:43.939
What we're trying to do is to understand where
are the hotspots, where are the urban heat

00:03:43.939 --> 00:03:49.400
islands to better plan and prioritize resources.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:54.230
Resources in Sierra Leone are limited, so
Eugenia Kargbo wants to focus on helping the

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:56.220
most vulnerable people first.

00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:00.499
That includes female vendors who are exposed
to the sun all day when selling their produce

00:04:00.499 --> 00:04:03.079
in uncovered markets without any shade.

00:04:03.079 --> 00:04:08.879
So what we've done is to work together with
those women and design a project which we

00:04:08.879 --> 00:04:12.159
call the market shade cover project.

00:04:12.159 --> 00:04:13.159
Basically it's a simple design.

00:04:13.159 --> 00:04:18.549
We use material that is heat reflective, that
doesn't absorb the heat to provide a shade

00:04:18.549 --> 00:04:21.329
cover for the women.

00:04:21.329 --> 00:04:25.050
The project will be implemented and launched
within the next few months.

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:31.080
Just even seeing the joy on their face when
I was talking to them about the project gave

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:38.030
me hope that definitely there's something
that can be done to help save lives and help

00:04:38.030 --> 00:04:47.270
improve the living condition of people who
are the most vulnerable.

00:04:47.270 --> 00:04:51.639
On a larger scale, the design of entire cities
can evolve to beat the heat.

00:04:51.639 --> 00:04:56.509
One of the solutions that Athens has been
trying to implement, which a lot of cities

00:04:56.509 --> 00:05:01.310
have tried to implement around the world,
is creating green corridors.

00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:05.990
And we have finalized the designs of three
green corridors in Athens.

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:10.080
Meanwhile, Freetown is currently cleaning
up garbage sites and converting them into

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:13.289
public gardens that are clean, safe and cool.

00:05:13.289 --> 00:05:19.289
They are also planting a million trees across
the city so that Freetown eventually becomes

00:05:19.289 --> 00:05:20.449
Tree-town.

00:05:20.449 --> 00:05:23.419
Santiago de Chile is taking a similar approach.

00:05:23.419 --> 00:05:28.199
We just funded a $2 million urban forest program.

00:05:28.199 --> 00:05:37.060
It's a really ambitious initiative that aims
to provide at least 30,000 trees for the city.

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:40.879
We never had that in the past before.

00:05:40.879 --> 00:05:43.600
Trees don't just provide shade and reflect
the sun's rays back up.

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:47.419
They also help lower the air temperature around
them by essentially sweating.

00:05:47.419 --> 00:05:51.169
They take up water from the ground, which
then gets put back into the air through a

00:05:51.169 --> 00:05:54.389
process called evapotranspiration.

00:05:54.389 --> 00:05:58.020
But they aren't the only form of vegetation
that can help cool down the air.

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:01.740
In places where there might not be enough
space for new trees, streets and roofs can

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:04.449
also be greened.

00:06:04.449 --> 00:06:08.919
Cities across the world, from Paris to London,
Bangkok to Singapore, have started incorporating

00:06:08.919 --> 00:06:12.960
green roofs or living walls in their urban
architecture.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:15.320
And Santiago de Chile is looking to do the
same.

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:19.490
They are about to start building a cool roof
on a public building.

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:25.819
We know that green roofs can provide many
benefits like cooling down the buildings,

00:06:25.819 --> 00:06:31.590
saving energy, but also helping people to
look at some green.

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:38.590
It can help with their mental health and also
provide a little biodiversity within the city.

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:43.880
So we are doing the first one in September
and the idea is to measure everything in order

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:48.900
to scale that project to the rest of the city
in the near future.

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:52.620
Experts believe cities also have to change
the way they construct new buildings to adapt

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:53.909
to the heat in the future.

00:06:53.909 --> 00:06:59.710
Our cities are built primarily with materials
that absorb solar energy, that are impermeable

00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:04.139
to water, so we don't get the sort of evaporative
benefits, and so we've basically designed

00:07:04.139 --> 00:07:08.099
our cities to trap heat, and that's what they're
doing.

00:07:08.099 --> 00:07:11.830
Using nature-based materials with high solar
reflectance is one idea.

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:16.160
Another is going back to using light-colored
materials for the outside of buildings – from

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:20.479
walls to roofs – something that southern
Europe has been doing for a long time.

00:07:20.479 --> 00:07:23.690
And now other cities across the globe can
adapt that idea.

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:30.040
We have a lot of informal communities, people
who live in houses built from zinc.

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:32.430
So maybe that's a starting point.

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:41.009
Seeing how we can provide white paint.

00:07:41.009 --> 00:07:46.610
It's kind of a basic solution, but the impact
is immense.

00:07:46.610 --> 00:07:50.930
Of course climate change caused by carbon
emissions is the real driver of extreme heat.

00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:55.139
We have to drastically cut our greenhouse
gas emissions in order to prevent a catastrophic

00:07:55.139 --> 00:07:56.759
level of global warming.

00:07:56.759 --> 00:08:01.590
But experts say that even then, so much damage
has already been done that we’ll also have

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:03.190
to learn to adapt.

00:08:03.190 --> 00:08:08.030
Projections show that things are only getting
worse from here.

00:08:08.030 --> 00:08:13.460
That's why we are addressing the problem now
in order to prepare us for the future.

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:21.289
We know that preparedness takes time and we
don't need to wait until a disaster or a heat

00:08:21.289 --> 00:08:24.169
wave hits us in the face in order to prepare
for that.

00:08:24.169 --> 00:08:28.330
The good news is, there's a lot we can do
and we have it at our fingertips.

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:33.580
This is less an innovation problem and more
of an adoption problem, and that means we

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:35.500
can do something quickly about it if we put
our minds to it.

